Modal + V1 will be used for each



<------------can/could------------->

                                                                [1]. Ability
Can → present ability

I can swim.
She can speak English.
They can solve this problem.

Could → past ability

I could swim when I was five.
She could speak English when she was young.
They could solve difficult problems in college.

So:

Present → can
Past → could

                                                                [2].Possibility

Could can express a possibility.

It could rain today.

= There is a possibility that it will rain.

She could be at home.

= Maybe she is at home.

Can can also express general possibility:

It can get very hot here.

This means it is possible in general.

Compare:

It can get very hot here.
→ General possibility.

It could get very hot today.
→ Possible on this particular occasion.

                                      
                                      | Use             | Can       | Could                |
                                      | --------------- | --------- | -------------------- |
                                      | Present ability | ✅        | —                    |
                                      | Past ability    | —         | ✅                    |
                                      | Permission      | ✅        | ✅ More polite        |
                                      | Request         | ✅         | ✅ More polite        |
                                      | Possibility     | ✅ General | ✅ Specific/uncertain |
                                      | Suggestion      | —         | ✅                    |






                                               <-------------------will/would------------->

1. Future
Will → future

Use will for something you expect or decide will happen in the future.

I will call you tomorrow.
She will come next week.
They will finish the work.

Will = future

2. Would → past form of will

When reporting what someone said in the past, will → would.

Direct speech:

She said, "I will come."

Reported speech:

She said that she would come.

Another example:

He said, "I will help you."
→ He said that he would help me.



<-----------------Conditional sentences --------------->

This is one of the most important uses of would.

If I had enough money, I would buy a car.

If she studied harder, she would pass the exam.

Here:

If + past tense → would + V1

Notice:

would buy ✅
would bought ❌
                            
                                  
                                  | Use                    | **Will**    | **Would**     |
                                  | ---------------------- | ----------- | ------------- |
                                  | Future                 | ✅           | —             |
                                  | Reported speech        | —           | ✅             |
                                  | Polite request         | Less polite | ✅ More polite |
                                  | Willingness            | ✅           | ✅             |
                                  | Hypothetical situation | —           | ✅             |
                                  | Conditional            | —           | ✅             |
                                  | Repeated past action   | —           | ✅             |




                        <--------------------shall/should------------------->

1. Shall → Future

Traditionally, shall is used with I and we to express the future.

I shall return tomorrow.
We shall meet again.

But in modern everyday English, will is much more common:

I will return tomorrow. ✅
We will meet again. ✅

So you will mostly see shall in formal, traditional, or legal English.

2. Shall → Suggestions / Offers ⭐

This is the most useful modern use of shall.

Use shall I...? and shall we...?

Offering to do something

Shall I open the window?

= Do you want me to open the window?

Shall I help you?


Should → Advice

This is one of the most important uses.

You should study every day.

You should get some rest.

She should practice speaking English.

Meaning: It is advisable/good to do it.

Negative:

You shouldn't waste your time.

4. Should → Expectation

Should can mean that something is expected to happen.

The train should arrive at 6 PM.

= I expect the train to arrive at 6 PM.

           <------Should have + V3 ⭐-------->

This is very important.

should have + past participle (V3) is used when something was advisable in the past but didn't happen, or when criticizing/regretting an action.

You should have studied harder.

Meaning: You didn't study enough, but it was the right thing to do.

I should have called her.

= It would have been better if I had called her.

He shouldn't have said that.

= Saying that was a mistak
